  "text": "**Solution 1 has the correct final answer, 12.** I checked this by reasoning rather than against a verified source, so my confidence is moderate.\n\n- **Solution 2 (answer 3)** is wrong. Its argument would exclude the paw and diamond, but the paw and diamond classes do have bounded clique-width. Its claim that $\\mathcal M=\\{P_4\\}$ is not supported.\n- **Solution 3 (answer 7)** is wrong. It concedes that its key step, that pan-free graphs have bounded clique-width, is unproven. It also treats the diamond as excluded via an unproven claim about layered wheels. Its final sum depends on whether the paw is in $\\mathcal M$.\n- **Solution 1 (answer 12)** gives the most complete argument. Its candidates are $P_4$, the paw and the diamond, with 3, 4 and 5 edges. Its sufficiency claims are plausible: $P_4$-free graphs are cographs, which have clique-width at most 2. The paw and diamond cases are argued less fully, but they match a classification I recall (the class of graphs with no induced subdivision of $H$ has bounded clique-width iff $H$ is an induced subgraph of $P_4$, the paw or the diamond).\n\nIf $K_3+K_1$ were also maximal, it would add another 3-edge graph, and the set of edge counts would still be $\\{3,4,5\\}$, so the sum stays 12.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
